{"duration_api_ms":42865,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"268755aa-e0fa-4890-a7c4-11e426c65a68","total_cost_usd":0.58361,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":36077,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":7942,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":36077,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7942,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":36077,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":36077},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":24205,"outputTokens":15,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.02428,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-haiku-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":7942,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":36077,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.55933,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"unknown"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"piece_id\":\"01\",\"title\":\"What Changed: Rangefinding Binoculars and Multi-Use Packs Reshape Carry Decisions\",\"question\":\"What changed? Which newly released or newly reviewed gear matters most to Mammoth competitors, and why?\",\"argument\":\"The most consequential recent change in the evidence is consolidation: a single rangefinding binocular can replace a separate binocular and laser rangefinder, and a new modular pack frame claims to carry heavy loads more comfortably. Both reduce the number of items a competitor must carry on foot, which is the central burden of a multi-day event.\",\"synthesis\":\"Reviewer Matt at Rokslide describes the Tract Toric LRF as combining binocular optics, a laser rangefinder, and ballistic computation in one device, and reports ranging out to 3000 yards-plus in most conditions. The same review notes that the device is a 'brick' with a 2 lb 5.5 oz weight and that rain and fog limit ranging, so the consolidation has a weight-and-conditions tradeoff that a competitor must weigh. Separately, Rokslide's KUIU Icon 6 review reports a 1.2-mile loop test at 510 vertical feet with a 28 lb day load and an added 64 lb simulated load, and the reviewer reports the Icon 6 measured 5 lb 12.5 oz with accessories. The reviewer's comparison that the Icon 6 carries heavy loads with less fatigue than the previous Icon line is a practitioner observation, but the reviewer also notes the new design is not backward compatible with earlier Icon packs, which matters for anyone already owning gear. Both items are new in 2026 and bear on the question of what has changed in the load a competitor carries. This brief is an inference that consolidation matters most; the evidence shows the products, not that they lower total burden at Mammoth.\",\"limitations\":\"Both sources are single-publisher reviews from ad-supported sites, and the Rokslide reviews do not disclose a Mammoth connection; the Toric LRF is positioned for elk hunting, not for Mammoth's specific ranges. No Mammoth organizer, past competitor, or lab test is in the evidence. The KUIU review's load test is a reviewer-designed comparison, not a standardized test, and the durability claim for the frame is manufacturer-stated and not independently confirmed here. The Toric LRF reviewer states long-term durability is untested. Packets are excerpts; omitted passages may contain qualifications.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"01-c1\",\"statement\":\"The Tract Toric LRF combines a 10x42 binocular with a laser rangefinder and ballistic computation in one device, and the reviewer reports ranging on trees almost instantaneously out to 3000 yards plus in most conditions.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/tract-toric-lrf-rangefinding-binocular-review/\",\"version_id\":\"220ed6eb0893442944fab61d7dad5202a7be74c9abe5e6d3b342f9fc0db3d2a2\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"The Toric LRF gives reliable ranges on trees almost instantaneously in Best target mode out to 3000 yards plus in most conditions.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"01-c2\",\"statement\":\"The reviewer reports the Toric LRF weighs 2 lbs. 5.5 ounces with an Outdoorsman's stud attached, and describes it as compact but not lightweight.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/tract-toric-lrf-rangefinding-binocular-review/\",\"version_id\":\"220ed6eb0893442944fab61d7dad5202a7be74c9abe5e6d3b342f9fc0db3d2a2\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"The Toric LRF tips the scale at 2 lbs. 5.5 ounces with an Outdoorsman’s stud attached.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"01-c3\",\"statement\":\"The reviewer reports that ranging in thick clouds worked only with partially obscured targets, and that in rain the rain/snow setting got ranges out to just under 1000 yards.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/tract-toric-lrf-rangefinding-binocular-review/\",\"version_id\":\"220ed6eb0893442944fab61d7dad5202a7be74c9abe5e6d3b342f9fc0db3d2a2\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"In rainy conditions, the rain/snow setting did help get ranges out to just under 1000 yards.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"01-c4\",\"statement\":\"The KUIU Icon 6 is not backward compatible with earlier Icon and Icon Pro/Pro LT packs, according to the manufacturer's design change as reported by the reviewer.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/\",\"version_id\":\"e05dabe7e03e73aeb70fd9c735e547f1271e3a12c4896b1ef987af9b31254b57\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"To reach these goals, KUIU started from scratch, which means the new Icon 6 packs and frames are not backward compatible with the older Icons, including the Pro and Pro LT models.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"01-c5\",\"statement\":\"In the reviewer's 1.2-mile loop test with 510 vertical feet, the Icon 6 was tested alongside Stone Glacier and Kifaru packs, with a 28 lb day load and an added 64 lb simulated elk quarter.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/\",\"version_id\":\"e05dabe7e03e73aeb70fd9c735e547f1271e3a12c4896b1ef987af9b31254b57\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"For this review, I made 1.2-mile loops (510 vertical feet gained and lost) with each pack loaded with 28 pounds of day hunting gear, followed by re-hiking the same loop with an additional 64 pounds of blasting media in a game bag to simulate an elk quarter.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"01-c6\",\"statement\":\"The KUIU Icon 6 is a newly released design that consolidates the Icon line with a new frame, and its heavy-load comfort is reported as an improvement over previous Icon packs, which the reviewer describes as getting uncomfortable under heavy loads.\",\"type\":\"inference\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/\",\"version_id\":\"e05dabe7e03e73aeb70fd9c735e547f1271e3a12c4896b1ef987af9b31254b57\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"I liked the weight and function of the newer packs, but still felt heavy loads got too uncomfortable, and the suspension just didn’t quite fit me right.\"}}]}]}\n{\"piece_id\":\"02\",\"title\":\"What Holds Up: Manufacturer Claims Tested Against Field Measurement\",\"question\":\"What holds up? Which manufacturer claims withstand independent testing and practitioner experience, and where does the evidence conflict?\",\"argument\":\"Manufacturer specifications hold up unevenly. Scope click values, rangefinder performance, and a pack's heavy-load comfort are each tested by someone other than the maker, and the tests show that some advertised values are off and some claims are supported in practice. Where a manufacturer's claim is a rating, a warmth figure, or a durability assertion, the independent evidence in this packet is thinner or runs in a different direction.\",\"synthesis\":\"The Accurate Shooter article describes a reader test in which a Leupold 6.5-20X50 M1 moved 1.000 inch per four clicks rather than the 1.047 inch of true MOA, which the reader reports as about a 5% error, and the same reader reports other scopes matching their printed value. Bryan Litz, quoted in the same article, says more than 50% of long-range trajectory error is scope adjustment error, which is an expert opinion rather than a measured result. This is a manufacturer-advertised value tested against field measurement, and it shows the value must be verified per unit. The Treeline Review sleeping bag review offers a different kind of check: the reviewer, who is an affiliate-model publisher, reports the Magma 30 was accurate to its listed temperature, 'if not a little warm,' with a tested lower limit of 24°F, and reports weight of 1 lb 10 oz in the Regular Wide size. Because the reviewer's temperature observations span only down to 33°F, the reviewer does not test the 30°F rating at its claimed limit, and the reviewer notes they would not push it much below freezing. The Vortex-sponsored podcast with Argali's Brad Brooks gives a manufacturer's own account of why sleeping bag ratings differ, saying there is no rule requiring companies to use a standard, and promoting comfort ratings over limit ratings. This is a manufacturer claim and not independent confirmation, and its treated-down-DWR claim rests on a self-described unscientific submersion test, so it should be presented as contested rather than settled. The conflict is that the sleeping-bag claim and the scope claim both rely on testing this packet cannot fully reproduce.\",\"limitations\":\"The scope test is a single reader's measurement reported in a community article, with no lab verification in this packet; the Argali podcast is a sponsored manufacturer segment hosted by a Mammoth sponsor and its claims are treated as manufacturer claims. The sleeping-bag review is a single affiliate-model source and the reviewer did not test the rating below 33°F. The Tract and KUIU reviews are ad-supported and single-source. The excerpts may omit qualifications or counterevidence; this brief does not infer absence of other testing from omission. No Mammoth participant testimony is in this packet, and no controlled lab results beyond the cited reader's measurement are present.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"02-c1\",\"statement\":\"A reader reports that the Leupold 6.5-20X50 M1 moved 4 clicks 1.000 inch rather than the 1.047 inch of true MOA, which the reader describes as about a 5% error.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://bulletin.accurateshooter.com/2026/10/how-to-find-your-scopes-true-click-values-with-box-test/\",\"version_id\":\"7a62836f185d8550938253297ef501eee2aac8e2783e7d4fa09298e86af41728\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"That is to say, 4 clicks moved POI 1.000″, rather than 1.047″ (true MOA). That’s about a 5% error.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c2\",\"statement\":\"The reader reports that click values vary by manufacturer, model, and individual unit, and that a Leupold 3.5-10 M3LR and a U.S.O. SN-3 tested dead on while other SN-3s and the 6.5-20X50 M1 did not.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://bulletin.accurateshooter.com/2026/10/how-to-find-your-scopes-true-click-values-with-box-test/\",\"version_id\":\"7a62836f185d8550938253297ef501eee2aac8e2783e7d4fa09298e86af41728\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"I’ve found the true click value varies not only by manufacturer, but by model and individual unit.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c3\",\"statement\":\"Bryan Litz states that in his experience more than 50% of trajectory prediction error at long range is scope adjustment error, which is an expert opinion and not a measured result in this packet.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://bulletin.accurateshooter.com/2026/10/how-to-find-your-scopes-true-click-values-with-box-test/\",\"version_id\":\"7a62836f185d8550938253297ef501eee2aac8e2783e7d4fa09298e86af41728\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"In my experience, more than 50% of the time error in trajectory prediction at long range is actually scope adjustment error.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c4\",\"statement\":\"The reviewer reports the Magma 30 was accurate to its listed temperature, 'if not a little warm,' with a tested lower limit of 24°F, while the coldest night the reviewer recorded was 33°F.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/rei-co-op-magma-30-sleeping-bag-review\",\"version_id\":\"4c742dc416136bc209dcfd4b570e69002f847ac2b7c81e9b48b874b0de19bfd8\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"I found the Magma to be accurate in its listed temperature, if not a little warm.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c5\",\"statement\":\"The Magma 30 is reported at 1 lb 10 oz in the Regular Wide size, according to the reviewer's specifications and testing summary.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/rei-co-op-magma-30-sleeping-bag-review\",\"version_id\":\"4c742dc416136bc209dcfd4b570e69002f847ac2b7c81e9b48b874b0de19bfd8\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"the 1 lb 10 oz weight in the Regular Wide is fairly light—especially for a bag that comes in wider sizes.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c6\",\"statement\":\"Argali's Brad Brooks says there is no requirement that sleeping bag companies follow a rule for temperature ratings, and that companies use different rating types, so two 20-degree bags can differ in warmth.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SlW6o3cO2rk\",\"version_id\":\"8dc6ddadaee9daac8d079ff5a89e70839084d84baa998a3573b1574edf167815\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s-0001\"}},{\"item_id\":\"podcast:c219e3c1-b26c-4c37-882b-759af3f892df\",\"version_id\":\"706d57c181a240186c295b4643878e60ca99444061848ae779b70d1546c83cba\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s-0041\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c7\",\"statement\":\"The Vortex-hosted podcast with Argali's Brad Brooks states that a treated-DWR down bag may lose warmth after repeated use, a claim the host attributes to the manufacturer's own perspective and not to independent data.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"podcast:c219e3c1-b26c-4c37-882b-759af3f892df\",\"version_id\":\"706d57c181a240186c295b4643878e60ca99444061848ae779b70d1546c83cba\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s-0094\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c8\",\"statement\":\"The KUIU Icon 6 heavy-load comfort is reported by the reviewer as improved, with the reviewer saying the Icon 6 'completely remedied' fatigue with heavy loads, though the reviewer's loop test is a single reviewer-designed comparison.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/\",\"version_id\":\"e05dabe7e03e73aeb70fd9c735e547f1271e3a12c4896b1ef987af9b31254b57\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"The Icon 6 completely remedied this for me, and brings the Icon 6 on the level with the very best frames available.\"}}]}]}\n{\"piece_id\":\"03\",\"title\":\"What Should Readers Do: Test Fit, Load, and Weather Before Trusting Specs\",\"question\":\"Would this gear be good, required, effective at Mammoth? What practical implications are supported, and what should readers test or watch next?\",\"argument\":\"Readers should test pack fit, load stability, and weather performance under their own loads before committing to any single item. The supported field evidence shows that fit and load behavior vary by body size, pack volume, and load weight, and that a heavier configuration exposes differences that light loads hide. Official Mammoth requirements are not in this packet, so no required-status judgment is made.\",\"synthesis\":\"The Ruck Authority GORUCK Rucker 5.0 review, from a reviewer who received the bag free and earns affiliate commissions, reports carrying a 20 lb plate with camera and drone gear for a week, and reports the shoulder straps pulled back without a hip belt, and that the bag rode stably with no hot spots. The same reviewer reports the bag bounced when heavily loaded running downhill for the Black Diamond Distance 15 is a separate review, but the Treeline Review Distance 15 review by a non-sponsored reviewer reports the pack bounces when heavily loaded running downhill and that a size large would not cinch down properly on a 5'9\\\" reviewer. Together these show that fit and load stability depend on size and load, and a reader should test the pack with a loaded-weight trial. The Treeline Magma 30 review reports the bag is bulky in smaller packs and that a quilt can save weight, which informs a choice between a bag and a quilt. The Ruck Authority GR1 review reports the GR1 has no chest strap or hip belt, and the reviewer notes missing the lumbar support compared with the Rucker 5.0, so a reader who carries heavy loads should test lumbar support before buying. The Argali-hosted podcast gives a manufacturer's account that sleeping bag ratings are not standardized, which supports a reader checking comfort and limit ratings, not the single number on the tag. Weather performance is untested in several of these sources, including the Rucker's water resistance, which the reviewer says he has not tested. Readers should watch for long-term durability and water-resistance data before trusting those claims.\",\"limitations\":\"No official Mammoth packing list or rule is in this packet, so required status is unspecified. Every pack and bag source is single-publisher, several are affiliate or ad-supported, and the GORUCK reviews are from a publisher where the reviewer received the product free and disclosed affiliate links. The Ruck Authority reviewer carried only 20 lb and reports no hip-belt testing on the Rucker 5.0. The Treeline reviewer's sizing observations are based on a 5'9\\\" and 165 lb body and may not transfer to other body sizes. No Mammoth competitor or organizer testimony is in this packet, and no controlled lab results are included. The excerpts may omit qualifications.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"03-c1\",\"statement\":\"The Ruck Authority reviewer reports the GORUCK GR1 lacks a chest strap and hip belt, and that without a chest strap the straps pulled the shoulders back a little with 20 pounds of load.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:SlW6o3cO2rk\",\"version_id\":\"8dc6ddadaee9daac8d079ff5a89e70839084d84baa998a3573b1574edf167815\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s-0005\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c2\",\"statement\":\"The Ruck Authority reviewer reports the Rucker 5.0 has a larger lumbar support of 30 mm thickness and that a 20 lb plate carried comfortably over about one week, with no hot spots or chafing reported.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:jVz1KOKvi-I\",\"version_id\":\"21ec9121eed29ffe19c5c9436e6fb37aea5b6f887f750c12e65180de26ca96fc\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s-0007\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c3\",\"statement\":\"The Ruck Authority reviewer discloses a free loan of the Rucker 5.0 from GoRuck and affiliate links, and notes that the optional hip belt costs extra.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:jVz1KOKvi-I\",\"version_id\":\"21ec9121eed29ffe19c5c9436e6fb37aea5b6f887f750c12e65180de26ca96fc\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s-0009\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c4\",\"statement\":\"The Treeline Review reviewer reports the Black Diamond Distance 15 bounces when heavily loaded while running downhill, and that a size large could not be cinched to stop bouncing for a 5'9\\\" reviewer.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/black-diamond-distance-15-backpack-review\",\"version_id\":\"72a94336aee944d2865b19aa22833a0909ed2257f9513c0bece6e9d6948f3840\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"When heavily loaded, it bounces as you run, especially when running downhill, but it’s comfortable enough even if you have to hike until you drink some of your water.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c5\",\"statement\":\"The Treeline Review reviewer reports that the Magma 30 is bulky in a smaller pack, and that the bag took up significant room in a 44 L pack.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/rei-co-op-magma-30-sleeping-bag-review\",\"version_id\":\"4c742dc416136bc209dcfd4b570e69002f847ac2b7c81e9b48b874b0de19bfd8\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"In a smaller bag, like my Gregory Amber 44L, the Magma 30 took up too much space packed loosely\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c6\",\"statement\":\"The Argali-hosted podcast states there is no requirement for sleeping bag companies to follow a standard for temperature ratings, so the rating on a bag is not standardized across manufacturers.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"podcast:c219e3c1-b26c-4c37-882b-759af3f892df\",\"version_id\":\"706d57c181a240186c295b4643878e60ca99444061848ae779b70d1546c83cba\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s-0042\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c7\",\"statement\":\"The Ruck Authority reviewer states GORUCK describes the Rucker 5.0 as highly water resistant, but the reviewer has not yet tested that claim in rain.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:jVz1KOKvi-I\",\"version_id\":\"21ec9121eed29ffe19c5c9436e6fb37aea5b6f887f750c12e65180de26ca96fc\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s-0006\"}}]}]}","ttft_ms":41623,"type":"result","duration_ms":41702,"uuid":"19ed1b71-ca96-478a-a349-2f1d919ff933","ttft_stream_ms":759,"time_to_request_ms":90,"first_content_frame_ms":760,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
